    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im studying for my diesel mech exam in munich and i'm a bit stuck on turbochargers. can u explain how the exhaust gas actually turns the turbine and how that leads to more air getting into the engine? specifically how the pressure increases in the manifold?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A turbocharger uses the energy from hot, high-velocity exhaust gases to spin a turbine wheel, which is connected by a shaft to a compressor wheel. As the exhaust gases blast past the turbine blades, they impart kinetic energy, causing the turbine to spin at very high RPMs. The spinning compressor then sucks in ambient air and forces it through the compressor blades, compressing it. This compression increases the pressure of the air, which is then fed into the engine's intake manifold. This forced induction of denser, higher-pressure air allows the engine to burn more fuel efficiently, leading to more power.",
      "source": "target",
